**Modular Automated Cloud Infrastructure Provisioner in C#**

Below is a simple implementation of a modular automated cloud infrastructure provisioner. It consists of three main components:

*   **Declarative Resource Parser**: Responsible for parsing JSON definitions of cloud resources.
*   **Dependency Graph Resolver**: Determines the creation order of resources via topological sorting.
*   **Execution Engine**: Simulates asynchronous resource deployment.

### Declarative Resource Parser

This parser reads infrastructure JSON definitions and converts them into C# objects.

#### `Resource.cs`

```csharp
public class Resource
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public ResourceType Type { get; set; }
    public List<string> DependsOn { get; set; }

    public Resource()
    {
        DependsOn = new List<string>();
    }
}

public enum ResourceType
{
    VirtualMachine,
    Network,
    Storage
}
```

#### `DeclarativeResourceParser.cs`

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

public class DeclarativeResourceParser
{
    public List<Resource> ParseResources(string jsonDefinition)
    {
        var resourceDefinitions = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<List<ResourceDefinition>>(jsonDefinition);
        var resources = new List<Resource>();

        foreach (var resourceDefinition in resourceDefinitions)
        {
            var resource = new Resource
            {
                Name = resourceDefinition.Name,
                Type = (ResourceType)Enum.Parse(typeof(ResourceType), resourceDefinition.Type),
                DependsOn = resourceDefinition.DependsOn
            };

            resources.Add(resource);
        }

        return resources;
    }

    private class ResourceDefinition
    {
        [JsonPropertyName("name")]
        public string Name { get; set; }

        [JsonPropertyName("type")]
        public string Type { get; set; }

        [JsonPropertyName("dependsOn")]
        public List<string> DependsOn { get; set; }
    }
}
```

### Dependency Graph Resolver

This resolver determines the creation order of resources via topological sorting.

#### `DependencyGraphResolver.cs`

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class DependencyGraphResolver
{
    public List<Resource> ResolveDependencies(List<Resource> resources)
    {
        var graph = new Dictionary<string, List<string>>();
        var inDegree = new Dictionary<string, int>();

        // Build graph and calculate in-degree of each resource
        foreach (var resource in resources)
        {
            if (!graph.ContainsKey(resource.Name))
            {
                graph[resource.Name] = new List<string>();
            }

            inDegree[resource.Name] = 0;
        }

        foreach (var resource in resources)
        {
            foreach (var dependency in resource.DependsOn)
            {
                graph[dependency].Add(resource.Name);
                inDegree[resource.Name]++;
            }
        }

        // Perform topological sorting
        var queue = new Queue<string>(inDegree.Where(x => x.Value == 0).Select(x => x.Key));
        var sortedResources = new List<Resource>();

        while (queue.Count > 0)
        {
            var resourceName = queue.Dequeue();
            var resource = resources.First(r => r.Name == resourceName);

            sortedResources.Add(resource);

            foreach (var dependentResource in graph[resourceName])
            {
                inDegree[dependentResource]--;
                if (inDegree[dependentResource] == 0)
                {
                    queue.Enqueue(dependentResource);
                }
            }
        }

        return sortedResources;
    }
}
```

### Execution Engine

This engine simulates asynchronous resource deployment.

#### `ExecutionEngine.cs`

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class ExecutionEngine
{
    public async Task DeployResources(List<Resource> resources)
    {
        var tasks = new List<Task>();

        foreach (var resource in resources)
        {
            tasks.Add(DeployResource(resource));
        }

        await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
    }

    private async Task DeployResource(Resource resource)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Deploying resource {resource.Name} of type {resource.Type}");

        // Simulate deployment time
        await Task.Delay(2000);

        Console.WriteLine($"Resource {resource.Name} deployed successfully");
    }
}
```

### Example Usage

Here's an example of how to use the provisioner:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        var jsonDefinition = @"[
            {
                ""name"": ""network"",
                ""type"": ""Network"",
                ""dependsOn"": []
            },
            {
                ""name"": ""storage"",
                ""type"": ""Storage"",
                ""dependsOn"": [""network""]
            },
            {
                ""name"": ""vm"",
                ""type"": ""VirtualMachine"",
                ""dependsOn"": [""network"", ""storage""]
            }
        ]";

        var parser = new DeclarativeResourceParser();
        var resources = parser.ParseResources(jsonDefinition);

        var resolver = new DependencyGraphResolver();
        var sortedResources = resolver.ResolveDependencies(resources);

        var engine = new ExecutionEngine();
        await engine.DeployResources(sortedResources);
    }
}
```

In this example, we define three resources: a network, a storage, and a virtual machine. The virtual machine depends on both the network and the storage. The provisioner will deploy the resources in the correct order (network, storage, virtual machine).
